WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.210
Moon colonization is something that humans
have dreamt about for centuries, and it’s

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:07.500
entirely possible, it’s just a matter of
cost.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:12.320
Bringing both humans and supplies the 234,000
miles (377,000 km) to the lunar surface costs

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.800
an enormous amount of money and it’s difficult
to justify the cost to taxpayers.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.390
So how much would it cost?

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:19.870
Well that’s a complicated question.

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:23.450
For the sake of simplicity, let’s say we’re
doing a one year return mission with four

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:25.720
astronauts using NASA technology.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.570
The first thing we’ll calculate is the cost
of getting there, and we’ll need a rocket.

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:33.970
The Saturn V is the only rocket to have ever
existed that is powerful enough to take humans

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:35.470
beyond low earth orbit.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:38.230
The problem is, all 15 of them are long retired.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:43.620
The Saturn V had a lunar payload capacity
of 90,000 pounds and a per launch cost of

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:51.530
$189 million 1971 dollars or $1,123,000,000
2016 dollars which works out to $12,500 per

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:53.090
pound transported to the moon.

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:57.760
That may seem like a lot, but that would mean
that the average American, weighting 159 pounds

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.760
(72 kg), could travel to the moon on technology
that existed when computers looked like this

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.580
for a mere 2 million dollars.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.740
I know you know that that’s not right.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.310
There are two problems in that calculation.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:11.080
First off, that doesn’t include research
and development costs.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:16.570
A staggering 6.4 billion dollars was spent
to develop the Saturn V, which is 38 billion

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:18.440
in 2016 dollars.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.560
So, considering that the Saturn V launched
13 times, if you include the R&amp;D costs in

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:25.320
the per-launch-cost, that works out to just
over $4 billion per launch or $44,000 per

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.560
pound ($97,000 per kg.)

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.870
But, to colonize the moon, you don’t just
need to get things to the moon, you need to

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:32.549
get them on the moon.

00:01:32.549 --> 00:01:36.580
The budget allocated to develop and build
12 lunar landers back in the Apollo days was

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:41.320
18 billion 2016 dollars or 1.5 billion dollars
per lander.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.430
Once again, the lunar landers are no longer
in service so we’re including the R&amp;D costs

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:46.799
in our calculations.

00:01:46.799 --> 00:01:50.750
We need two landers to bring our four astronauts
to the moon and these have to be able to return

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.800
so they need to land with both launch systems
and fuel.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:58.420
So that’s 1.5 billion per lander plus 4
billion per Saturn V launch so 11 billion

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.960
total to carry our astronauts to the moon
and back.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.460
During the Apollo days, NASA also developed
a module called the LM Truck which was designed

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.600
to deliver payloads to the surface of the
moon without a crew.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:13.200
Not only could these land 11,000 pounds (5,000
kg) of cargo, but a Saturn V rocket could

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.889
launch with two of them.

00:02:14.889 --> 00:02:19.159
Using the truck, we can bring our per pound
cost of bringing cargo to the lunar surface

00:02:19.159 --> 00:02:22.040
down to a reasonable $318,000.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.370
Believe it or not, that’s a bargain.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.939
So, once our four astronauts get to the moon,
they need somewhere to live.

00:02:27.939 --> 00:02:32.340
The Center for Strategic and International
Studies in Washington, DC did a super detailed

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.540
study on how much the development and construction
of a lunar base would cost.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:41.349
For our four person base, it would cost $20
billion to develop plus one of our $4 billion

00:02:41.349 --> 00:02:43.310
dollar launches equalling $24 billion.

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:44.840
That’s a little steep.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:49.379
For a budget option, we could go with something
like Bigelow Airspace’s B330 spacecraft.

00:02:49.379 --> 00:02:53.669
These are essentially inflatable buildings
that work in space, and with a bit of modification

00:02:53.669 --> 00:02:56.640
they could conceivably be altered to work
on the surface of the moon.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.159
Bigelow Airspace is a commercial company,
so they’ve said that the B330 would rent

00:03:00.159 --> 00:03:05.329
out for $1.25 million per day or $465 million
for our one year mission.

00:03:05.329 --> 00:03:08.519
Considering that that’s the first time I’ve
said million when talking about a current

00:03:08.519 --> 00:03:12.549
day cost, that’s practically nothing…
until you consider the problem of getting

00:03:12.549 --> 00:03:14.309
the B330 to the moon.

00:03:14.309 --> 00:03:19.529
It weighs 43,000 pounds so it’d take four
LM Truck landings and two Saturn V launches

00:03:19.529 --> 00:03:21.859
for a total of $14.5 billion dollars.

00:03:21.859 --> 00:03:24.469
Well, that’s a little better than $24 billion.

00:03:24.469 --> 00:03:26.230
Let’s move on to food.

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:29.849
For food, the best, but not simplest, solution
is to just grow it.

00:03:29.849 --> 00:03:34.439
One lunar greenhouse developed by the University
of Arizona could produce about 5.5 pounds

00:03:34.439 --> 00:03:35.659
of food per day.

00:03:35.659 --> 00:03:39.309
In order to have variety, NASA would probably
choose a few different crops that would do

00:03:39.309 --> 00:03:40.709
well on the moon.

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:45.389
For our calculations, we’ll say the greenhouse
would be filled with 76% sweet potato, 16%

00:03:45.389 --> 00:03:46.819
tomato, and 10% strawberry.

00:03:46.819 --> 00:03:51.799
That’ll come out to 4.3 pounds of sweet
potato, .8 pounds of tomato, and .4 pounds

00:03:51.799 --> 00:03:53.389
of strawberries per day.

00:03:53.389 --> 00:03:59.669
Sweet potatoes have 389 calories per pound
or 1,675 calories per day per greenhouse.

00:03:59.669 --> 00:04:03.779
The tomatoes (81) and strawberries (151) will
make 65 and 60 calories per day per greenhouse

00:04:03.779 --> 00:04:07.669
respectively, so that’s 1,800 calories per
greenhouse per day total.

00:04:07.669 --> 00:04:11.739
Each of our four astronauts would need about
3,000 calories per day equalling 12,000 total

00:04:11.739 --> 00:04:15.970
per day so we’ll need 6.67 greenhouses and
since you can’t really bring two thirds

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:19.610
of a greenhouse we’ll bring seven and have
surplus food, which is great for backup.

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:24.290
Each lunar greenhouse will require .2 pounds
of fertilizer per day which adds up to 1.4

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:28.500
pounds of fertilizer per day for our seven
greenhouses and 510 pounds for the year.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:32.370
So for the greenhouse growing scheme we’ll
need to physically bring the greenhouses which

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:38.780
each weigh a full metric ton or 2,200 pounds
which for the seven of them is 15,400 pounds

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:44.200
or $4.9 billion dollars in transport costs,
plus another $162 million for fertilizer.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.940
The International Space Station is a really
good example of how water resources could

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:49.290
be managed on the moon.

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:51.700
Every bit used on the ISS is recycled.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:55.570
That includes water from condensation, showers,
sweat, and yes, urine.

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:59.990
It of course gets filtered heavily, but the
water in astronauts food is the same that

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:03.050
came from… you know… other stuff.

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:08.110
NASA says that, without water recycling, 40,000
pounds of water would be needed to sustain

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:09.710
four astronauts on the moon.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:13.050
That would cost 12.7 billion dollars to transport.

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:18.440
With water recycling, we can bring that down
to 5,110 pounds of water for the year… except

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:19.830
we have our greenhouse.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:24.850
Quite a bit of water is needed to grow the
plants—6920 pounds worth to be specific.

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:31.410
So that’s 12,030 pounds of water for the
year which would cost $3.825 billion to bring.

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:34.840
So next we need air… as in oxygen to breathe.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:39.860
A human at moderate activity levels needs
1.85 pounds of oxygen per day, so that means

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:44.960
we’d need 7.4 pounds of oxygen per day for
our four astronauts, 2,701 pounds for the

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:48.420
year, which would cost $860 million, but wait!

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:52.700
Because of our greenhouses and the amazing
process of photosynthesis, we don’t!

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:58.440
One lunar greenhouse produces .75 pounds of
oxygen per day so our seven create 5.25 pounds

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:04.520
of oxygen each day, so we only need to bring
2.15 pounds of oxygen per day, or 785 pounds

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.120
for the year, which will cost a mere $250
million.

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:11.760
You may ask why we don’t use a oxygen scrubber,
and the simple answer is that it would cost

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.070
quite a bit more to develop, build, and deliver
a scrubber capable of creating enough clean

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:17.600
air for our crew of four.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:21.580
It’s just cheaper and less risky to bring
all the necessary oxygen there.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:25.050
In terms of power, it’s been proposed that
the best way to bring electricity to the moon

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:27.150
is to literally bring a nuclear reactor.

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:31.300
It’d be a little one, but it would carry
out fission reactions on the surface of the

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:32.300
moon.

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:35.890
NASA has only proposed, not developed, this
system, but they said that it could be developed,

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:40.080
flight-tested, and delivered to the moon for
only $1.4 billion.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:42.120
Now we have everything we need.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:46.240
So to answer the question you came here for:
How much would it cost to live a year on the

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:47.240
moon?

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:51.460
I’ll just say before giving my answer that
these were all estimates and far from perfect.

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:55.980
For example, it’s likely that if NASA ever
actually colonized the moon, it’d be decades

00:06:55.980 --> 00:07:00.330
in the future when commercial space companies
are far more developed and the cost of transport

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:01.640
would be much less.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:06.120
Spaceflight Industries already sells cargo
space to lunar orbit for a mere $10 million

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:07.700
per 220 pounds (100kg.)

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:11.800
With prices that low, it may not be long until
living on the moon or other celestial bodies

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:15.170
becomes commercially advantageous through
mining and tourism.

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:19.510
The very day this video goes up, Elon Musk
is anticipated to announce Space X’s plans

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:25.370
to get to mars, including a massive rocket
that will be able to carry as many as 220,000

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:30.450
pounds to Mars for less than any previous
rocket which could make martian colonization

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.010
economically ffeasible.

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:37.170
So after adding everything up, my conservative
estimate for one year with four astronauts

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:40.540
on the moon comes out to $36 billion dollars.

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:50.560
That’s $98 million dollars per day, $4.1
million per hour, $68,300 per minute, or $1,138

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:55.400
per second—more than an average family makes
in a year in Madagascar.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.460
Is it worth it—maybe.

00:07:57.460 --> 00:08:01.360
If we ever have to leave earth because of
the environmental change that we’ve started,

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:05.400
that $36 billion dollars might be the best
investment we’ve ever made.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:10.860
After all, living on the Moon or Mars or some
other celestial body is a crucial first step

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:14.940
in learning how to survive when earth is no
longer.

